  "id": "DrugBank_DB00465 - MONDO_5642",
  "verbalization": "Ketorolac, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, is shown to have a potential role in the treatment of atopic conjunctivitis through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Ketorolac may be associated with vernal conjunctivitis, a type of allergic conjunctivitis, which in turn is linked to atopic conjunctivitis. This relationship is supported by the presence of multiple indications that connect Ketorolac to different agents, including alcaftadine, emedastine, and tromethamine, all of which are indicated for atopic conjunctivitis. The shared indication of vernal conjunctivitis as a precursor to atopic conjunctivitis implies a possible mechanistic link, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The evidence is consistent with a potential role of Ketorolac in modulating inflammatory pathways, though further investigation is needed to confirm the direct or indirect effects on the immune system or ocular inflammation. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of considering the broader pharmacological context in evaluating the therapeutic potential of Ketorolac for atopic conjunctivitis.",
